<!-- ============================ 01 BLACKBODY ============================ -->
<section class="exp" id="blackbody">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">01</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1900</span><span class="chip">Max Planck</span><span class="chip">Berlin</span></div>
        <h2>Blackbody Radiation &amp; the Birth of the Quantum</h2>
        <p class="lede">Every object glows. The color and brightness of that glow obeyed no known law —
        until a clerk at the Berlin Patent Office made a desperate, brilliant guess.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>A hollow cavity heated to temperature <b>T</b> emits "blackbody" radiation through a tiny hole.
          Classical physics — the Rayleigh–Jeans law — says each electromagnetic mode should carry <b>kT</b> of
          energy, and the modes pile up without bound toward the ultraviolet. The prediction: an infinitely hot
          sky. This was the <b>ultraviolet catastrophe</b>.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>The real spectrum climbs, peaks, and falls off. Planck's fit worked only if the cavity's oscillators
          could exchange energy in discrete packets <b>E = nhν</b>. He called it "an act of desperation". It was
          the birth of the quantum.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>A single constant, <b>h = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ J·s</b>, entered physics. Raise the temperature and the peak
          slides exactly as Wien's law demands; the dashed classical curve meanwhile races off toward the
          ultraviolet — the catastrophe, on display.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Key relations</span><code>E = hν</code><code>B(λ,T) = 2hc²/λ⁵ · 1/(e^(hc/λkT) − 1)</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">heated cavity + spectrum</span></div>
        <canvas id="cBlackbody" class="sim"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="bbTemp">Cavity temperature <output id="bbTempOut">5800 K</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="bbTemp" min="300" max="12000" step="25" value="5800"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <div class="sw-row">
              <span class="ctl-label">Classical prediction (Rayleigh–Jeans)</span>
              <label class="switch"><input type="checkbox" id="bbRJ" checked/><span></span></label>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">Temperature <b id="bbStatT">5800 K</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Wien peak λmax <b id="bbStatL">500 nm</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 02 PHOTOELECTRIC ============================ -->
<section class="exp flip" id="photoelectric">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">02</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1905</span><span class="chip">Albert Einstein</span><span class="chip">Zürich / Berlin</span></div>
        <h2>The Photoelectric Effect &amp; the Photon</h2>
        <p class="lede">Shine light on a clean metal plate and electrons pop off. The classical wave story made
        one prediction that simply did not happen.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>If light is a wave, its energy comes from intensity: dim light should eventually eject electrons and
          no threshold should exist. But below a cutoff frequency <b>ν₀</b>, <b>no</b> light of any brightness frees
          an electron — and above it, electrons leave instantly with energies that are independent of brightness.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>Einstein's 1905 move: light arrives in quanta — <b>photons</b> — each carrying energy <b>hν</b>. A
          photon gives its whole energy to a single electron: <b>K<sub>max</sub> = hν − φ</b>. Intensity changes
          only how many photons arrive, so it scales the current, never the energy per electron.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>The photon was real. This effect runs every photodiode, solar cell and light sensor you own.
          Push the frequency below the red tick (ν₀) and the current dies — crank the intensity to maximum and
          it stays dead. That single fact killed wave-only light.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Key relation</span><code>K<sub>max</sub> = hν − φ</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">lamp → cathode</span></div>
        <canvas id="cPhoto" class="sim"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="peF">Frequency ν <output id="peFOut">550 THz</output></label>
            <div class="range-wrap">
              <input type="range" id="peF" min="100" max="1500" step="5" value="550"/>
              <span class="tick" id="peTick"></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="peI">Intensity <output id="peIOut">60 %</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="peI" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="60"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="peMetal">Cathode metal</label>
            <select id="peMetal">
              <option value="0">Cesium — φ = 2.14 eV</option>
              <option value="1">Sodium — φ = 2.28 eV</option>
              <option value="2">Zinc — φ = 4.31 eV</option>
              <option value="3">Platinum — φ = 6.35 eV</option>
            </select>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">Photon energy hν <b id="peE">2.28 eV</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Work function φ <b id="pePhi">2.14 eV</b></div>
          <div class="stat">K<sub>max</sub> <b id="peK">0.14 eV</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Stopping voltage <b id="peV">0.14 V</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="meter-wrap">
          <label>Photoelectric current <output id="peCur">—</output></label>
          <div class="meter"><i id="peMeter"></i></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 03 COMPTON ============================ -->
<section class="exp" id="compton">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">03</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1923</span><span class="chip">Arthur Compton</span><span class="chip">Washington University</span></div>
        <h2>Compton Scattering: Photons With Momentum</h2>
        <p class="lede">If photons carry energy, they must carry momentum too. Compton found a collision that
        proved it — head-on, in plain sight.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>X-rays scatter off the loose electrons in graphite. A classical wave should emerge with essentially
          the <b>same wavelength</b>. Yet the scattered X-rays were measurably <b>longer</b>, and the shift
          depended only on the scattering angle — no classical model predicted such a clean relation.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>The shift obeys <b>Δλ = (h/mc)(1 − cosθ)</b> — exactly the bookkeeping of a particle collision: a
          photon with momentum <b>h/λ</b> trades energy and momentum with a free electron. Rotate the detector
          and the shift follows the formula to four significant figures.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>Compton's 1923 result settled the debate: light is quantized in its <b>momentum</b>, not just its
          energy. It underpins X-ray and neutron diffraction, and it is the cleanest single demonstration that
          "particle" and "wave" are not mutually exclusive.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Key relation</span><code>Δλ = (h/mc)(1 − cosθ) = 2.426 pm · (1 − cosθ)</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">photon → free electron</span></div>
        <canvas id="cCompton" class="sim"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="cmLi">Incident wavelength <output id="cmLiOut">0.150 nm</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="cmLi" min="0.05" max="0.5" step="0.005" value="0.15"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="cmTh">Scattering angle θ <output id="cmThOut">90°</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="cmTh" min="0" max="180" step="1" value="90"/>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">λ in <b id="cmIn">0.150 nm</b></div>
          <div class="stat">λ out <b id="cmOut">0.153 nm</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Shift Δλ <b id="cmDL">2.4 pm</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 04 STERN–GERLACH ============================ -->
<section class="exp flip" id="stern">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">04</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1922</span><span class="chip">Otto Stern · Walther Gerlach</span><span class="chip">Frankfurt</span></div>
        <h2>Stern–Gerlach: The Beam That Split in Two</h2>
        <p class="lede">Point a beam of silver atoms through an uneven magnetic field. Classical physics predicts
        a smear. Nature writes two dots.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>Silver atoms carry one unpaired electron and thus a magnetic moment. Classically that moment can point
          in <b>any</b> direction, so passing through a gradient field should fan the beam into a
          <b>continuous</b> band on the detector screen.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>In 1922 Stern and Gerlach saw exactly <b>two spots</b>: spin-up, spin-down. Angular momentum is
          quantized — measured along any axis it only ever returns <b>±ħ/2</b>. Send the beam through a second
          analyzer rotated 90° and the two spots split <b>again</b>, 50/50, as if the first measurement were
          forgotten.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>Spin-½ is the single most important two-level system in physics: the <b>qubit</b>, MRI, electron
          spin resonance, atomic clocks. Toggle the second analyzer below to see the surprise that keeps quantum
          information honest.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Key result</span><code>S<sub>z</sub> = ± ½ħ</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">atomic beam → inhomogeneous field</span></div>
        <canvas id="cStern" class="sim"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="sgRate">Beam rate <output id="sgRateOut">4 /s</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="sgRate" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="4"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="sgG">Field gradient <output id="sgGOut">60 %</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="sgG" min="15" max="100" step="5" value="60"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <div class="sw-row">
              <span class="ctl-label">Second analyzer (axis x)</span>
              <label class="switch"><input type="checkbox" id="sgS2"/><span></span></label>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">Spot ↑ <b id="sgUp">0</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Spot ↓ <b id="sgDown">0</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Ratio ↑ : ↓ <b id="sgRatio">— : —</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 05 DAVISSON–GERMER ============================ -->
<section class="exp" id="davisson">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">05</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1927</span><span class="chip">C. Davisson · L. Germer</span><span class="chip">Bell Labs</span></div>
        <h2>Electron Diffraction: Matter Waves Confirmed</h2>
        <p class="lede">Louis de Broglie bet the physics community that electrons have wavelengths. Two years
        later a lab accident in New Jersey proved him right.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>de Broglie (1924): every particle with momentum <b>p</b> has a wavelength <b>λ = h/p</b>. An electron
          accelerated through voltage V has <b>λ = h/√(2meV)</b>, so a beam of electrons should diffract off a
          crystal exactly like X-rays, satisfying Bragg's law <b>nλ = 2d sinθ</b>.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>Davisson and Germer fired electrons at a nickel target — an oxygen-baked accident had grown a giant
          single crystal. At just the right voltage a <b>sharp peak</b> appeared at the Bragg angle, and as the
          voltage rose the peak slid exactly where the matter-wave formula demanded. (G. P. Thomson got the same
          result the same year, through thin films.)</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>Matter is a wave. Electron microscopes, low-energy electron diffraction and half of condensed-matter
          physics rest on it. Sweep the voltage and watch the diffraction peak hunt its angle.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Key relations</span><code>nλ = 2d sinθ</code><code>λ = h/√(2meV) = 1.226/√V nm</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">electron gun → Ni crystal → detector arc</span></div>
        <canvas id="cDavis" class="sim"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="dvV">Accelerating voltage <output id="dvVOut">54 V</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="dvV" min="10" max="100" step="1" value="54"/>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">de Broglie λ <b id="dvL">0.167 nm</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Bragg angle θB <b id="dvTB">22.3°</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Lattice spacing d <b>0.215 nm</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 06 DOUBLE SLIT ============================ -->
<section class="exp flip" id="slits">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">06</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1989</span><span class="chip">Tonomura, Tonomura group &amp; predecessors</span><span class="chip">Hitachi, Japan</span></div>
        <h2>The Double Slit: Dots That Add to a Wave</h2>
        <p class="lede">The entire content of quantum mechanics in one image: dots, that add up to a wave.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>Fire electrons, <b>one at a time</b>, through two slits. Each lands as a single dot — a particle.
          But the dots accumulate into bright and dark <b>interference fringes</b> — a wave. How does one dot
          "know" about the other slit?</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>Tonomura's 1989 version made it vivid: the fringe pattern builds up dot by dot, from single-particle
          impacts. Now turn on a <b>which-path detector</b>: the fringes collapse into two plain bands, exactly
          classical. Measurement does not merely observe the quantum world — it reshapes it.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>This is the measurement problem in its purest form, and the reason "wavefunction" and "collapse" are
          still debated a century later. Let the pattern build for a minute — it is the most beautiful image in
          physics.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Interference</span><code>I(x) ∝ cos²(πdx/λL) · sinc²(πax/λL)</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">single-electron buildup</span></div>
        <canvas id="cSlits" class="sim tall"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="slRate">Emission rate <output id="slRateOut">90 /s</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="slRate" min="5" max="300" step="5" value="90"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="slSep">Slit separation <output id="slSepOut">1.00 ×</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="slSep" min="0.5" max="2" step="0.05" value="1"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="slWid">Slit width <output id="slWidOut">1.00 ×</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="slWid" min="0.5" max="2" step="0.05" value="1"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <div class="sw-row">
              <span class="ctl-label">Which-path detector</span>
              <label class="switch"><input type="checkbox" id="slWP"/><span></span></label>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <div class="sw-row" style="align-items:flex-end">
              <span class="ctl-label" style="opacity:.7">Pattern</span>
              <button class="chipbtn" id="slClear">Clear screen</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">Particles landed <b id="slN">0</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Emergent pattern <b id="slState">interference fringes</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 07 HYDROGEN ============================ -->
<section class="exp" id="hydrogen">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">07</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1885 → 1913</span><span class="chip">Balmer · N. Bohr</span><span class="chip">Copenhagen</span></div>
        <h2>The Hydrogen Spectrum: Orbits Become Levels</h2>
        <p class="lede">The simplest atom in the universe emits only certain colors of light. No one could
        explain why — until orbits became energy levels.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>Hydrogen's spectrum is a set of razor-sharp lines (Balmer series: 410, 434, 486, 656 nm) that fit
          Balmer's 1885 formula with <b>no theory</b> behind it. Why those wavelengths and no others?</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>Bohr's 1913 atom: electrons live only in quantized levels <b>E<sub>n</sub> = −13.6 eV/n²</b>. When an
          electron falls between levels it emits a photon of <b>exactly</b> that energy difference — every line in
          the spectrum is a transition. Excite the atom and watch the Balmer lines build on the right: the same
          pattern etched into every nebula photograph.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>Bohr's circular orbits were soon replaced by Schrödinger's orbitals, but the level structure
          survived. Atomic clocks, lasers and all of spectroscopy run on quantized transitions like these.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">Key relations</span><code>E<sub>n</sub> = −13.6 eV / n²</code><code>hν = E<sub>i</sub> − E<sub>f</sub></code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">click an orbit or excite with a button</span></div>
        <canvas id="cHyd" class="sim tall"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
