              <button class="chipbtn" id="hb4">n = 4</button>
              <button class="chipbtn" id="hb5">n = 5</button>
              <button class="chipbtn" id="hb6">n = 6</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <div class="sw-row">
              <span class="ctl-label">Automatic decay</span>
              <label class="switch"><input type="checkbox" id="hAuto" checked/><span></span></label>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">Current level <b id="hN">n = 4</b></div>
          <div class="stat">Last emission <b id="hLine" style="font-size:12px">—</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================ 08 BELL ============================ -->
<section class="exp flip" id="bell">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="exp-head reveal">
      <span class="exp-index">08</span>
      <div>
        <div class="exp-meta"><span class="chip year">1935 → 1982</span><span class="chip">EPR · Bell · Aspect</span><span class="chip">Paris</span></div>
        <h2>Bell's Theorem: Spooky Action, Measured</h2>
        <p class="lede">Einstein called it "spooky action at a distance". Bell turned the complaint into an
        inequality — and experiments broke the inequality.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="exp-grid">
      <article class="prose reveal">
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>The puzzle</h4>
          <p>Two entangled photons fly to Alice and Bob, who measure polarization at freely chosen angles. The
          EPR camp argued: the results must be decided in advance by hidden variables, and nothing influences a
          distant lab. Bell (1964) showed any such <b>local</b> theory must obey <b>|S| ≤ 2</b> for a clever
          combination of four correlation settings.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>What was observed</h4>
          <p>Quantum mechanics predicts the singlet correlation <b>E = −cos 2(a−b)</b>, which reaches
          <b>|S| = 2√2 ≈ 2.83</b> at the optimal settings. Aspect's 1982 experiment — and every loophole-hardened
          experiment since — measured the quantum value. Local realism is not just incomplete; it is <b>false</b>.</p></div>
        <div class="prose-block"><h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <p>Entanglement is now an engineering resource: quantum key distribution, teleportation and quantum
          computers. Drag the analyzer angles to feel the correlation curve, then run the CHSH protocol and watch
          the classical bound get broken.</p></div>
        <div class="eq"><span class="eq-label">The test</span><code>|S| ≤ 2 (local)</code><code>|S| = 2√2 (Q.M.)</code></div>
      </article>
      <div class="simcard reveal">
        <div class="simcard-head"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive apparatus<span class="sim-hint">entangled pair → two polarizers</span></div>
        <canvas id="cBell" class="sim tall"></canvas>
        <div class="controls">
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="blA">Alice's angle a <output id="blAOut">0°</output></label>
            <input type="range" id="blA" min="0" max="180" step="1" value="0"/>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label class="ctl-label" for="blB">Bob's angle b <output id="blBOut">45°</output></label>
